  "body": {
    "id": "prop:three-horizon-nonidentification-transfer",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "In the certified three-horizon construction \\(\\mathfrak E_3\\), the full laws represented by \\(q^-_{ihj}\\) and \\(q^+_{ihj}\\) are distinct, satisfy \\(D(0)\\leq D(1)\\), and induce the same observable distribution because both have within-arm laws \\(\\mu_0^\\star\\) and \\(\\mu_1^\\star\\) and the same treatment and censoring mechanisms.  Nevertheless their projected couplings are \\(f^-\\) and \\(f^+\\), with \\(\\ell_\\star(f^-)=-1/20\\) and \\(\\ell_\\star(f^+)=1/4\\).  Hence every nonparametric causal model class containing both laws is non-point-identified for \\(\\ell_\\star\\) at that common observed distribution.  This upward transfer is a nonidentification statement only; it does not assert that the marked-capacity image remains sharp after terminal monotonicity is removed.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\mathfrak E_3",
      "q^-_{ihj}",
      "q^+_{ihj}",
      "D(z)",
      "\\mu_0^\\star",
      "\\mu_1^\\star",
      "f^-",
      "f^+",
      "\\ell_\\star"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:primitive-three-horizon-exhibit",
      "prop:strict-cross-cohort-support",
      "ass:consistency"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The finite-table certificate in the strict-support proposition gives\n\\[\n \\left(\\sum_hq^-_{ihj}\\right)_{ij}=f^-_{ij},\\qquad\n \\left(\\sum_hq^+_{ihj}\\right)_{ij}=f^+_{ij},\\qquad\n \\left(\\sum_iq^-_{ihj}\\right)_{hj}\n =\\left(\\sum_iq^+_{ihj}\\right)_{hj}=\\mu_{1,hj}^\\star.                                    \\tag{7}\n\\]\nAfter attaching \\(R(0)=\\infty\\), both laws have control margin \\(\\mu_0^\\star\\).  Every positive entry listed in the construction has \\(i\\leq h\\), so both laws satisfy \\(D(0)\\leq D(1)\\).  They are distinct: for example,\n\\[\n f^-_{00}=1/10\\neq1/20=f^+_{00}.                                                           \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe construction uses the same degenerate covariate, \\(Z\\sim\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(1/2)\\) independent of the potential path, and \\(C=\\tau=3\\) under both signs.  By consistency, treatment arm \\(z\\) reveals only \\((D(z),R(z))\\).  Thus, for every measurable set \\(A\\) of observed paths, if \\(\\mathcal O(z,d,r,3)\\) denotes the deterministic observed history generated from treatment \\(z\\), event times \\((d,r)\\), and censoring time \\(3\\), then (7) gives\n\\[\n \\Pr_\\pm(O\\in A)\n =\\frac12\\sum_{z=0}^1\n   \\int\\mathbf 1\\{\\mathcal O(z,d,r,3)\\in A\\}\\,d\\mu_z^\\star(d,r),                       \\tag{9}\n\\]\nwhich is independent of the sign.  This also verifies conditional exchangeability directly, while \\(e=1/2\\) and \\(G_z(u)=1\\) supply treatment and full-horizon censoring overlap.\n\nThe same finite-table proposition computes\n\\[\n \\ell_\\star(f^-)=-\\frac1{20},\n \\qquad\n \\ell_\\star(f^+)=\\frac14.                                                                \\tag{10}\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathfrak C\\) be any nonparametric causal class containing both constructed laws.  At the common observable distribution in (9), the identified set for \\(\\ell_\\star\\) over \\(\\mathfrak C\\) contains both values in (10); by (10) they differ by \\(3/10>0\\).  Therefore \\(\\ell_\\star\\) is not point identified in \\(\\mathfrak C\\).  Enlarging a model class can only add compatible full laws, so the two-law witness survives every such enlargement.  This set-inclusion argument does not show that all additional, possibly nonmonotone laws are represented by \\(\\mathcal N_x\\), and accordingly proves no enlarged-class sharpness claim.",
    "justification": "The two-law certificate transfers nonidentification to every larger causal class containing the certified lifts without falsely transferring sharpness of the monotonicity-restricted capacity image.",
    "gap": "A strict support calculation inside the monotone class does not by itself say what survives when that class is enlarged; equality of the induced observed laws supplies the required upward-transfer witness.",
    "consumer": "Applied users who decline terminal monotonicity still obtain a certified nonidentification conclusion, but not the monotonicity-restricted sharp capacity characterization.",
